      "problem": "${(s-t)}^{9}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u2026+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(s-t)}^{9},$ we have  $a=s,$ $b=-t,$ $n=9.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(s-t)}^{9}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(s-t)}^{9} & =(\\frac{9}{0}){(s)}^{9}{(-t)}^{0}+(\\frac{9}{1}){(s)}^{9-1}{(-t)}^{1}+(\\frac{9}{2}){(s)}^{9-2}{(-t)}^{2}+\u2026 \\\\  & =1{(s)}^{9}+9{(s)}^{8}{(-t)}^{1}+36{(s)}^{7}{(-t)}^{2}+\u2026 \\\\  & =1({s}^{9})+9({s}^{8})(-t)+36({s}^{7})({t}^{2})+\u2026 \\\\  & ={s}^{9}-9{s}^{8}t+36{s}^{7}{t}^{2}+\u2026\\end{aligned}\n$$",
